Exercise 1 – Basic implementation

Create an interface Vehicle with:

one abstract method int getMaxSpeed()

Create a class Car that implements Vehicle.

Task

Implement the interface correctly

Verify that the method in Car is public

Try (mentally or in code) to instantiate Vehicle and observe why it fails

Exercise 2 – Multiple interfaces

Create two interfaces:

Flyable with method void fly()

Sailable with method void sail()

Create a class AmphibiousPlane that implements both interfaces.

Task

Implement both methods

Confirm that one class can implement multiple interfaces

Exercise 3 – Duplicate method conflict (compile-time error)

Create two interfaces:

Readable with method int read()

Writable with method double read()

Create a class Device that implements both interfaces.

Task

Try to implement the class

Identify exactly why the code does not compile

Do not fix it yet — just understand the rule being violated

Exercise 4 – Default method usage

Create an interface Logger with:

one abstract method String getPrefix()

one default method void log(String message)
(the default method should use getPrefix())

Create a class FileLogger that implements Logger.

Task

Implement only the abstract method

Call log() from an instance of FileLogger

Observe how the default method uses the class implementation

Exercise 5 – Overriding default methods

Extend the previous exercise.

Create another class ConsoleLogger implementing Logger.

Task

Override the default log method

Call both the overridden and original behavior (hint: you cannot call the default directly yet — think why)

Exercise 6 – Default method conflict

Create two interfaces:

AutoPilot with default method void navigate()

ManualControl with default method void navigate()

Create a class Car that implements both interfaces.

Task

Observe the compilation error

Override navigate()

Inside the override, explicitly call both interface implementations using InterfaceName.super.methodName()

Exercise 7 – Private methods inside interfaces

Create an interface Validator with:

a default method boolean validate(String input)

a private method used inside validate

a private static helper method

Task

Use the private methods to split validation logic

Confirm where each private method can be called from

Exercise 8 – Abstract methods used by default methods

Create an interface ReportGenerator with:

abstract method String fetchData()

default method void generateReport() that uses fetchData()

Create a class DatabaseReportGenerator.

Task

Implement only fetchData()

Call generateReport()

Explain why this works even though the default method calls an abstract one

Exercise 9 – Interface vs abstract class thinking exercise

Create:

an interface Identifiable

an abstract class BaseEntity

Task

Decide what belongs in the interface and what in the abstract class

Justify your decision (no code needed, just reasoning)